## 2. What went wrong before, and where each mistake came from

### 2.1 The three versions

| Version | Idea | Problem it ran into |
|---|---|---|
| v1: scikit-learn `MLPClassifier` | One token at a time. Input = hashed character n-grams of the previous, current and next token. Output class = **the corrected word**. | Closed vocabulary; row-level splits leaked; unequal-length sentence pairs skipped. |
| v2: PyTorch window classifier | Same idea, bigger: ±3 token window, residual MLP, GPU, group split, `<copy>/<del>/word` labels. | Honest evaluation exposed the closed-vocabulary problem. Sentence results were no better than "do nothing". |
| v3 (this file) | Two stages: open-vocabulary speller + sentence-level tagger with shareable edit labels, tuned thresholds. | Not yet measured (see status note). |

### 2.2 The measured results of v2 (the only real numbers in this README)

```
words/target_reachable            0.0618     <- only 6% of correct words existed as output classes
words/accuracy                    0.0015     (do-nothing 0.0027)
sentence_tokens/accuracy          0.8436     (do-nothing 0.8526)   <- worse than doing nothing
sentence_tokens/edit_recall       0.1327
sentences/char_error_rate         0.1648     (do-nothing 0.1556)   <- worse than doing nothing
best epoch                        5 of 11    <- overfitting early
demo: deleted "Yesterday", "alot", "Wen"     <- harmful deletions
```

### 2.3 Root causes (why each number looked that way)

**(a) Spelling was framed as classification over a closed set of words.**
The output layer had one neuron per corrected word seen in training. A classifier can only output a
class it has a neuron for. With a *group split* (all misspellings of a word kept together, which is the
honest way to test), almost every test word is by definition a word that never appeared as a class:
`target_reachable = 0.0618` means 93.8% of test words were impossible to get right, whatever the
network learned. The earlier row-wise split had hidden this, because the same correct word appeared
in both train and test. This was not a bug in training. It was a flaw in the *problem formulation*.

**(b) The sentence data is tiny, and the labels were lexical.**
`go → went` and `walk → walked` and `jump → jumped` were each a separate class with a handful of
examples. With roughly 2,000 sentences, nearly every edit class was too rare to learn, so edit recall
was 13%.

**(c) Severe class imbalance.**
About 85% of tokens need no change (do-nothing accuracy 0.8526). A model can minimise cross-entropy
by almost always predicting "keep". Any model has to beat 0.8526, and v2 did not.

**(d) Too little context.**
A ±3 token window cannot see `Yesterday` from a verb far away. Tense is a sentence-level property.

**(e) A loose confidence rule produced harmful deletions.**
`<del>` predictions passed a 0.30 confidence threshold. Some `<del>` labels came from alignment noise
(a deleted token in one pair is a legitimate keep in another), so the model learned deletions from
noisy evidence and applied them confidently enough to pass 0.30.

**(f) A large model on small data.**
A 65k-bucket word embedding plus 512-wide blocks has enough capacity to memorise ~16k real training
tokens. Best validation loss at epoch 5 of 11 is the classic signature.

**(g) Data-pipeline leaks and losses in v1.**
- Row-level splitting put tokens of the same sentence into different splits (their windows overlap,
  so the model effectively saw parts of test sentences).
- Pairs with different token counts were skipped, which silently removed exactly the run-together /
  split-word errors (`alot`, `to day`) that matter for dyslexia.
- Everything was lowercased and never restored.
- Apostrophe-less negations (`dident`, `dont`) were not protected by the negation guard, which only
  knew `didn't` and friends.

**(h) GPU looked idle.**
Per-step `.item()` calls forced the CPU and GPU to synchronise every step, the model per step was tiny,
and most wall-clock time was single-threaded Python (tokenizing, aligning, hashing).

