## 11. Evaluation: what is measured and how to read it

All numbers are produced on the **held-out test split**. Always compare with the "do nothing"
baselines in the same table.

### 11.1 Speller table (`evaluate_speller`)

Evaluated on up to 1,000 randomly chosen (seeded) test word pairs, words the model never saw:

| Column | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `do_nothing` | accuracy of returning the typed word unchanged |
| `model_only` | the network alone (no dictionary/rules) |
| `library_only` | pyspellchecker alone |
| `hybrid` | the real pipeline stage (network + dictionary + rules) |
| `hybrid_keeps_correct_words` | feed the *correct* words in; fraction left unchanged. This is the "harm" measure. |

You want `hybrid ≥ max(model_only, library_only)` and `hybrid_keeps_correct_words` near 1.

### 11.2 Pipeline table

Token level (tagger, with all guards):

| Metric | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `accuracy` / `do_nothing_accuracy` | fraction of tokens with the right label; the baseline is the fraction needing no edit |
| `edit_recall` | of tokens that needed an edit, the fraction fixed *exactly right* |
| `edit_precision` | of edits made, the fraction exactly right |
| `false_edit_rate` | of tokens that needed no edit, the fraction wrongly edited |
| `target_reachable` | fraction of true labels that exist as classes (the ceiling on accuracy) |
| `negations_broken` | count of negation tokens whose output lost the negation; should be 0 |

Whole sentence (`spell_only`, `best`, `last`): `exact_match` (normalised sentence equals the reference),
`char_error_rate` (Levenshtein distance between normalised sentences divided by the reference length),
each with its `do_nothing_*` baseline. The `spell_only` column shows what the tagger adds beyond
spelling.

### 11.3 Demo sentences

`DEMO_SENTENCES` has 10 hand-written sentences covering spelling, phonetics, negation, tense,
agreement, homophones, run-together words and one already-correct sentence (it must be unchanged). It is
a qualitative look, not a measurement.

