## 14. Improvements, ranked by effort

### Easy (an hour or less, within the current architecture)

| Improvement | Why / expected effect |
|---|---|
| **Fix the fuzzy-negation tie-break**: choose the candidate with the smallest edit distance, ties broken alphabetically | Removes run-to-run non-determinism. |
| **Extend `NEGATIONS`** (`nobody, nothing, none, neither, nor, without, hardly`) and skip capitalised mid-sentence tokens | Wider meaning protection. |
| **Exclude LayerNorm/bias/embeddings from weight decay** (two optimizer parameter groups) | Standard, usually small gain. |
| **Increase `ngram_buckets`** (e.g. 2^18) if memory allows | Fewer hash collisions. |
| **Extend `CONFUSION_GROUPS`** (more homophones and irregular verbs) | Directly widens what the tagger can learn. |
| **Mine confusion pairs automatically**: from aligned 1-to-1 replacements in your sentence file where *both* words are dictionary words, add them to the confusion lookup | Learns your data's own real-word errors for synthetic generation. |
| **Look at `report.json` histories**: `grad_norm`, train vs val loss | Verifies the optimisation claims on real runs. |
| **Tune `copy_loss_weight`, `synthetic_variants`, `synthetic_error_prob`, `tag_dropout`** | The main levers for the tagger. |
| **Apply the tagger twice** (re-run on its own output) | Fixes dependent errors (iterative correction as in GEC systems). Cheap to try. |

### Medium (a day)

| Improvement | Why / expected effect |
|---|---|
| **Beam search / n-best in the speller + rerank by word frequency** (noisy-channel: `log P_net + λ log P_freq`) | Repairs the uniform-prior caveat; `wen → when` becomes principled. |
| **Context-aware spell reranking**: score each speller candidate by the tagger or a small word-level language model trained on your correct sentences | Resolves `when/then`. Needs the candidate list from the beam. |
| **Measure your own error statistics**: align your word pairs at character level, count edit types, and sample synthetic noise from the *empirical* distribution instead of the hand-written table | Makes the synthetic noise match your real writers. |
| **More clean sentences for synthetic generation** (any clean English text corpus is data, not a pretrained model) | Multiplies the tagger's training signal. Most effective improvement for the tagger's weakest part. |
| **Temperature scaling** on validation logits | Better-calibrated confidences, thus better thresholds. |
| **Separate thresholds per label type** (`SUF`, `REPL`, `DEL`) | Different error costs per type. |
| **Checkpoint averaging** (average weights of the last few epochs) | Usually a free quality gain. |

### Larger

| Improvement | Why / expected effect |
|---|---|
| **Sentence-level seq2seq for the grammar stage** with a copy mechanism | Handles insertions and reordering; needs far more data. |
| **CRF / sequence-level output for the tagger** | Consistent label sequences. |
| **A real dyslexic-writing test set** (even 200 pairs hand-collected) | The only honest measure of the actual goal. |
| **Standard GEC metrics** (M²/ERRANT-style F0.5) | Comparable to published work (note: ERRANT's tooling uses pretrained NLP models, so check your constraint). |
| **Train a small word-level language model from your own correct text** for reranking | In-spirit "from scratch" substitute for pretrained LMs. |

